            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582783,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Early Life and Education</strong><br>\nFrancis Crick was born on June 8, 1916, in Northampton, England. From a young age, he was fascinated by science and the natural world. He studied physics at University College London and earned a Bachelor of Science degree in 1937. His research was interrupted by World War II, during which he worked as a scientist for the British Admiralty, mainly involved in designing magnetic and acoustic mines.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Transition to Biology</strong><br>\nAfter the war, Crick transitioned from physics to biology, which was uncommon at the time. He was convinced that the tools of physics and chemistry could be applied to solve biological problems. In 1947, Crick began his studies at the Strangeways Research Laboratory in Cambridge, where he focused on the physical properties of cytoplasm.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Collaboration with James Watson</strong><br>\nIn 1951, Crick began working with James Watson at the University of Cambridge's Cavendish Laboratory. Their partnership was dynamic and fruitful, as they shared the goal of discovering the structure of DNA. Watson's knowledge of genetics combined with Crick's understanding of x-ray diffraction techniques, which they applied to study DNA.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "53bc5361-5ce5-43a9-a00f-57bbceee9cd0"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
