      {
        "elements": [
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 4.415011037527593,
            "width": 37.98882681564246,
            "height": 8.830022075055187,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><span style=\"font-size:1.5em;\"><strong>INFORMATION TEXT (Part 2)</strong></span></p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "55aaf4d3-f7e3-4a3e-9196-01a6f7474f94"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
            "displayAsButton": false,
            "buttonSize": "big",
            "goToSlideType": "specified",
            "invisible": false,
            "solution": ""
          },
          {
            "x": 2.23463687150838,
            "y": 13.245033112582782,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Discovery of the DNA Structure</strong><br>\nCrick and Watson, aided by Rosalind Franklin's x-ray diffraction images of DNA, proposed the double helix structure of DNA in 1953. This was a groundbreaking moment in biology as it explained how DNA replicates and carries genetic information. Their model showed that DNA is made of two strands twisted into a helix, with bases inside like rungs on a ladder.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Further Research and Nobel Prize</strong><br>\nThe discovery of the DNA structure was just the beginning for Crick. He continued to study molecular biology and made significant contributions to understanding the genetic code. In 1962, Crick, Watson, and Maurice Wilkins were awarded the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine for their work on the structure of DNA.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Expanding the Field of Genetics</strong><br>\nCrick's later work played a pivotal role in the growth of molecular genetics. He conducted research that helped scientists understand the relationship between DNA, RNA, and protein synthesis. His work laid the foundation for modern genetic research and biotechnology.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
                "contentType": "Text",
                "license": "U",
                "title": "Unbenannt: Text",
                "defaultLanguage": "en"
              },
              "subContentId": "3b13867b-feab-4e2e-bfa2-fcc82e4dbff1"
            },
            "alwaysDisplayComments": false,
            "backgroundOpacity": 0,
