          {
            "x": 2.178649237472767,
            "y": 12.910611231371064,
            "width": 92.73743016759778,
            "height": 86.09271523178808,
            "action": {
              "library": "H5P.AdvancedText 1.1",
              "params": {
                "text": "<p><strong>Consciousness and Neurobiology</strong><br>\nIn his later career, Francis Crick's interest shifted towards neuroscience and the study of consciousness. He moved to the Salk Institute in California in 1977, where he explored the biological basis of consciousness. He was curious about how the brain could create a conscious mind and spent his later years researching this subject.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Personal Life</strong><br>\nOutside of his scientific endeavors, Crick was known to be a vibrant and outspoken figure. He was passionate about science and its implications for society. Crick was married twice and had three children. He was also an avid reader and enjoyed a wide range of interests, including gardening and art.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Legacy</strong><br>\nFrancis Crick passed away on July 28, 2004, leaving behind a towering legacy. He is remembered as one of the most influential biologists of the 20th century. His work not only revolutionized our understanding of genetics but also transformed the fields of biology and medicine. His scientific curiosity and cross-disciplinary approach continue to inspire scientists around the world.</p>\n"
              },
              "metadata": {
